the target language is: prolog.

% The two warnings of `; memorable` (docs/user/reference/language.md §2.4).
% The program runs, and its answers are right; the verifier says where the
% marker does not pay, or is not safe:
%
% - memorable_under_negation: "a person is childless" calls the memorable
%   template under `it is not the case that`. A negation stops at the first
%   answer it finds, while a memorable call computes every answer before
%   giving the first — wasted work, unless the same call is also made
%   outside the negation (as `descendants` does here for Elizabeth).
%
% - memorable_calls_prolog: the rule for "a person is on record" runs an
%   embedded `prolog` goal. Such a goal could change the state the answers
%   were computed from, and the cached answers would not know.

the templates are:
    *a person* is a parent of *a child*.
    *an ancestor* is an ancestor of *a person*; memorable.
    *a person* is childless.
    *a person* is on record; memorable.

the knowledge base checks includes:

an ancestor is an ancestor of a person if
    the ancestor is a parent of the person.

an ancestor is an ancestor of a person if
    a parent is a parent of the person
    and the ancestor is an ancestor of the parent.

a person is childless if
    the person is on record
    and it is not the case that
        the person is an ancestor of a descendant.

a person is on record if
    a parent is a parent of the person
    and prolog atom(the person).

scenario tudors is:
    Henry is a parent of Elizabeth.
    Henry is a parent of Edward.
    Elizabeth is a parent of Robert.
    descendants expects answers ["Elizabeth is an ancestor of Robert"].
    childless expects answers ["Edward is childless", "Robert is childless"].

query descendants is:
    Elizabeth is an ancestor of which person.

query childless is:
    which person is childless.
